868
01:45:41.260 --> 01:45:45.350
Justin Traglia: I'm gonna revert that change, and we can add it back if necessary later. Potuz?

869
01:45:46.010 --> 01:45:58.099
Potuz: I think something we could explore is something along the lines of, say we have deterministically four aggregators, we order them, and we have them send in the aggregate at different times.

870
01:45:58.650 --> 01:46:01.850
Potuz: And we make them all as late as possible.

871
01:46:02.280 --> 01:46:12.829
Potuz: So that we expect them all to have all the attestations already, and then we make the later aggregators not send anything if their aggregates are not substantially better.

872
01:46:13.750 --> 01:46:17.909
Potuz: So we make the honest aggregator behavior not to spandon the chain.

873
01:46:19.590 --> 01:46:24.929
Justin Traglia: That's an interesting idea. Do you think 100 milliseconds between the aggregates would be enough?

874
01:46:25.740 --> 01:46:27.049
Potuz: I, I, I…

875
01:46:27.560 --> 01:46:36.559
Potuz: I don't know, the P2P crowd would have to tell me, but I would suspect not, because you want… you want the aggregators to see the previous aggregate.

876
01:46:39.920 --> 01:46:40.560
Justin Traglia: Okay.

877
01:46:42.350 --> 01:46:49.140
Justin Traglia: Maybe $2.50 with 4 aggregators would be the last second of… That, like, period.

878
01:46:53.010 --> 01:46:54.760
Justin Traglia: Would that be sufficient, you think?

879
01:46:56.470 --> 01:46:59.130
Justin Traglia: It… Kev, it is, but there's a…

880
01:47:00.050 --> 01:47:04.090
Justin Traglia: An assumption that the later aggregators will not send.

881
01:47:04.260 --> 01:47:05.559
Justin Traglia: their aggregates.

882
01:47:05.700 --> 01:47:11.370
Justin Traglia: If they're not substantially better, like, 5%, or, like… 5% more steak or something.

883
01:47:11.690 --> 01:47:28.520
Potuz: But it's also not the same, right? Because on the best aggregator method, you still get all of the bandwidth taken, right? I mean, every aggregator that sends later sends a better aggregate, and those things propagate. So you do get a lot of repeated things being propagated.

884
01:47:28.520 --> 01:47:29.090
Justin Traglia: Yep.

885
01:47:29.400 --> 01:47:34.480
Potuz: If the aggregators themselves, they self-censor, because they have seen a better aggregate.

886
01:47:34.730 --> 01:47:38.279
Potuz: Then… then we avoid even sending those.

887
01:47:39.410 --> 01:47:48.339
Kevaundray Wedderburn: I thought that's what we was, doing. So, like, if Justin sends me his aggregates, and his one's better than mine, I drop mine,

888
01:47:49.090 --> 01:47:50.080
Kevaundray Wedderburn: And just use.

889
01:47:50.080 --> 01:47:50.930
Justin Traglia: Yeah, you would've…

890
01:47:51.240 --> 01:47:57.659
Justin Traglia: you would have already… like, if you're… if you're an aggregator as well, you would have already sent yours. Like, we would send them probably around the same time.

891
01:47:58.720 --> 01:47:59.929
Kevaundray Wedderburn: Oh, it's… oh, okay.

892
01:47:59.930 --> 01:48:01.380
Justin Traglia: The network would…

893
01:48:01.680 --> 01:48:10.979
Justin Traglia: like, as a whole, drop the worst aggregate eventually. So it won't make its way to all notes, but it would make it to probably, like, a large percentage of them, or…

894
01:48:11.140 --> 01:48:13.230
Justin Traglia: 10% or something, I don't know.

895
01:48:13.670 --> 01:48:23.960
Potuz: So, I think it's fine. So, we don't want to have, like, 250 milliseconds, probably, for this, because, because you need this aggregate to propagate across the whole chain, right?

896
01:48:24.210 --> 01:48:41.199
Potuz: To get… I mean, it needs to go from one aggregator to another specific node. So, probably you want that number to be larger, say, like, 500 milliseconds, and it's fine if the last aggregator sends very late, because it's not so urgent that that aggregate gets on time.

897
01:48:44.950 --> 01:48:49.429
Mikhail Kalinin: It's not that urgent if we had the first one before.

898
01:48:49.600 --> 01:49:03.890
Potuz: Exactly. And so… so as long as the first few go and are surely reaching attesters on time, then we're fine. And also, keep in mind that these aggregates are important that they reach not at second 12th.

899
01:49:04.180 --> 01:49:09.019
Potuz: So that's for the proposer. The proposer, in principle, could, for example, register to all subnets.

900
01:49:10.310 --> 01:49:16.260
Potuz: So, it's not so important that these aggregates are ready at 2nd 12th, they need to be ready at 2nd 15.

901
01:49:17.360 --> 01:49:20.520
